The table structure information is (table names): 
['player', 'team', 'match', 'player_match', 'ball_by_ball', 'batsman_scored', 'wicket_taken', 'extra_runs']
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Relevant tables / columns and how they would be used  

player  
• player_id : primary key, joins to every other cricket‑related table.  
• player_name : to display the player’s name.  
• batting_hand : show whether the player is a right/left‑hand batter.  
• bowling_skill : show the player’s bowling style.  

player_match  
• match_id, player_id : allow us to know in which matches a player appeared (to count total matches).  
• role : “Captain”, “Keeper”, “Allrounder”, etc.  We can GROUP BY player_id, role and pick the most‑frequent role.  
• team_id : helps in joins if a player changes teams.  

batsman_scored  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no : composite key, lets us join to ball_by_ball / wicket_taken to keep deliveries aligned.  
• runs_scored : actual runs credited to striker – needed for (i) total career runs, (ii) runs per match in order to find highest score, 30+ / 50+ / 100+ counts.  

ball_by_ball  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no : composite key.  
• striker : to count balls a player faced (for strike rate).  
• bowler  : to attribute every legal ball (and thus all runs in batsman_scored) to the bowler – required for runs‑conceded and economy rate.  

wicket_taken  
• match_id, over_id, ball_id, innings_no : aligns with ball_by_ball; join to discover which bowler produced the dismissal.  
• player_out : gives the dismissed batter – needed to count dismissals for batting average.  
• kind_out   : can be ignored for requested output, but may filter run‑outs if wanted.  

extra_runs (ignored)  
• We were told to ignore extras, so economy rate will use only runs_off_bat (batsman_scored).  

Typical conditions / joins  
1. player_match AS pm  JOIN player p  ON p.player_id = pm.player_id     (to count matches / roles)  
2. batsman_scored bs JOIN ball_by_ball bb ON (bs.match_id = bb.match_id AND bs.over_id = bb.over_id AND bs.ball_id = bb.ball_id AND bs.innings_no = bb.innings_no)  (to attribute each scoring shot to both the striker and the bowler).  
3. wicket_taken wk JOIN ball_by_ball bb ON the same keys, then bb.bowler = some_player  (to count wickets for each bowler).  
4. wk.player_out = p.player_id  (to count times a batter got out).  

Below are ten exploratory queries (LIMIT 20) that move from simple inspection toward parts of the final computation.  

```sql
-- 1. Description: Show 20 sample players.
SELECT "player_id", "player_name", "batting_hand", "bowling_skill"
FROM "player"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2. Description: List the distinct roles used in the competition.
SELECT DISTINCT "role"
FROM "player_match"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3. Description: How many matches has each player appeared in? (first 20)
SELECT "player_id",
       COUNT(DISTINCT "match_id") AS "matches_played"
FROM "player_match"
GROUP BY "player_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4. Description: Total runs scored by the first 20 players (career aggregate).
SELECT bs."striker"   AS "player_id",
       SUM(bs."runs_scored") AS "total_runs"
FROM "batsman_scored" AS bs
GROUP BY bs."striker"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5. Description: Total dismissals for each batter (first 20).
SELECT wk."player_out" AS "player_id",
       COUNT(*)        AS "times_out"
FROM "wicket_taken" AS wk
GROUP BY wk."player_out"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6. Description: Balls faced by a player; join ball_by_ball to isolate striker. (first 20 players)
SELECT bb."striker" AS "player_id",
       COUNT(*)     AS "balls_faced"
FROM "ball_by_ball" AS bb
GROUP BY bb."striker"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7. Description: Highest score per player‑match (top 20 rows).
SELECT bs."striker"  AS "player_id",
       bs."match_id",
       SUM(bs."runs_scored") AS "runs_in_match"
FROM "batsman_scored" AS bs
GROUP BY bs."striker", bs."match_id"
ORDER BY "runs_in_match" DESC
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8. Description: Wickets taken by each bowler (first 20 players who bowled a wicket).
SELECT bb."bowler" AS "player_id",
       COUNT(*)    AS "wickets"
FROM "wicket_taken"  AS wk
JOIN "ball_by_ball"  AS bb
  ON wk."match_id" = bb."match_id"
 AND wk."over_id"  = bb."over_id"
 AND wk."ball_id"  = bb."ball_id"
 AND wk."innings_no" = bb."innings_no"
GROUP BY bb."bowler"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9. Description: Runs conceded by a bowler in each match (top 20).
SELECT bb."bowler" AS "player_id",
       bb."match_id",
       SUM(bs."runs_scored") AS "runs_off_bat_conceded"
FROM "ball_by_ball"  AS bb
JOIN "batsman_scored" AS bs
  ON bb."match_id" = bs."match_id"
 AND bb."over_id"  = bs."over_id"
 AND bb."ball_id"  = bs."ball_id"
 AND bb."innings_no" = bs."innings_no"
GROUP BY bb."bowler", bb."match_id"
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 10. Description: A near‑final combined query for one sample player (id = 1) summarising batting and bowling headline numbers.
SELECT p."player_id",
       p."player_name",
       SUM(bs."runs_scored")                            AS "career_runs",
       COUNT(DISTINCT pm."match_id")                    AS "matches_played",
       COUNT(wk."player_out")                           AS "dismissals",
       COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE bb."bowler" = 1)          AS "balls_bowled",
       COUNT(wk.*)  FILTER (WHERE bb."bowler" = 1)      AS "wickets"
FROM "player"          AS p
LEFT JOIN "player_match"   AS pm ON p."player_id" = pm."player_id"
LEFT JOIN "batsman_scored" AS bs ON p."player_id" = bs."striker"
LEFT JOIN "wicket_taken"   AS wk ON p."player_id" = wk."player_out"
LEFT JOIN "ball_by_ball"   AS bb ON p."player_id" = bb."bowler"
WHERE p."player_id" = 1
GROUP BY p."player_id", p."player_name"
LIMIT 20;
```

These queries collectively confirm the data needed for computing every requested career statistic and illustrate the necessary joins among the available tables.
